[h1]Rats Radio[/h1]
Makes the radio net sound like people talking instead of a screen reader, and has your units tell you why they aren't doing what you ordered. Turn the sound on: [url=https://youtu.be/pGwOIPXckpY]video of the radio net in a mission[/url].

[h2]Plans: more languages[/h2]
Right now every unit speaks English. The plan is for units to speak their own nation's language, with each language as a separate Workshop item that plugs into this mod:
[list]
[*]First, voice pack support, so the language follows your side. English stays in this mod and nothing changes for NATO players.
[*]Then Русский (Russian) for the Soviet navy and naval aviation, with Soviet callsigns.
[*]Then Español (Spanish) for the South Atlantic 1982 update: Argentina, plus Chile, Cuba and Spain.
[*]Later 中文 / 日本語 (Chinese, Japanese).
[/list]

[h2]Voices[/h2]
[list]
[*]43 voices based on real speakers, men and women with American, Canadian, English, Scottish, Irish, Northern Irish, Australian and New Zealand accents.
[*]Each of your ships, submarines, aircraft and helicopters gets its own voice. That includes aircraft launched from your carriers and airbases during the mission.
[*]Ships use their name ("Iowa", "Pargo"), aircraft their squadron callsign from the game ("Lancer 3-2"), everything else gets a generated NATO callsign ("Viper two-one", "Sentinel").
[*]Voices are fixed per mission, so after a reload everyone sounds the same as before.
[*]Each line is cut from a full spoken sentence, so callsign, phrase and numbers (bearings, track numbers, altitudes, counts) run together without a gap before the number.
[/list]

[h2]How the net behaves[/h2]
[list]
[*]The stock voice lines are replaced: order acknowledgements, fox and missile away calls, splashes, hits, incoming missiles, launches, sonar and ROE. The unit that triggered the line is the one who says it.
[*]A unit gives its callsign when it takes the net. If the same unit keeps talking it skips it, and a wingman just says "two, fox three".
[*]Urgent calls (missile inbound, we're hit, bingo) cut in. Replies to your orders go ahead of routine reports and come through louder and cleaner. Calls that are too old get dropped, because a late "copy" is more confusing than none, and when a whole flight acknowledges you hear it twice at most.
[*]Radio sound: band-limited, compressed voice, carrier hiss, mic click, crackle and a squelch tail, made fresh for every transmission.
[*]Everything plays on the game's voice channel, so the voice volume slider still works.
[/list]

[h2]Units explain refusals[/h2]
The game works out every frame why an order can't be carried out, but never tells you. With this mod the unit says it on the radio and in a message:
[list]
[*]"Unable, target not in rear aspect (IR missile)", "outside launch envelope", "missile cannot reach the target", "inside minimum range"
[*]"Negative, no radar lock", "no sensor track", "sonar contact only", "target below the radar horizon", "no line of sight"
[*]"Too low / too high to launch", "Turning, target not in the firing arc", "wrong speed for launch", "not cleared to engage", "seeker can't see the target"
[*]"Reloading, ready in four minutes", "nothing left for that target", "defending against a missile"
[*]"Order dropped, no sensor can see the target" when the game silently deletes your order
[*]A wingman told to VID who stays in formation says so. Optionally he breaks formation and does it.
[/list]

[h2]Order queue (F8)[/h2]
A small window with the selected unit's queued attack orders, each with its own delete button. The game only has cease fire, which throws away the whole queue. Each order shows what it's waiting for, like the weapon's state or the reload time left.

[h2]Contact reports[/h2]
[list]
[*]New contacts are reported by the unit whose sensor found them, with the bearing. Several at once are one call, and a raid that shows up aircraft by aircraft is still one call.
[*]A contact is only called lost after it has stayed lost for a while (20 s by default), and at most every two minutes per contact. Contacts that keep flickering get called "intermittent" once.
[*]Hostile classifications say what identified them (visual, ESM, sonar or radar), and the log names the unit and sensor.
[*]Neutral traffic is optional.
[/list]

[h2]Clickable messages[/h2]
[list]
[*]Every unit and track number in a message is a link that moves the camera there, in the bottom right and in the message log. The game itself only links one or two.
[*]A call's message stays up until it has been spoken, and the one being spoken has a marker.
[/list]

[h2]Air operations[/h2]
[list]
[*]Joker, bingo and fuel emergency calls, worked out against the nearest deck or airfield that can actually take the aircraft. Bingo never comes later than the point where the game sends it home. This also covers aircraft the game gives no home base call at all, which otherwise just run dry and fall out of the sky.
[*]Aircraft heading home are yellow on the map and in the formation manager, and returning groups move to the bottom of the list.
[*]Search and rescue: survivors in the water with a bearing (once per group of rafts), rescuer on the way, pickup complete with souls aboard, cabin full, survivors delivered.
[/list]

[h2]Formation manager[/h2]
Each unit gets a line under it saying what it is, in the game's own words and language: Destroyer, Missile Cruiser, Attack Submarine, Aircraft Carrier, ASW Helicopter, or for aircraft the mission they're loaded for (CAP, Strike, SEAD, Anti Ship, ASW, AEW). Groups show the role their members share.

[h2]Settings[/h2]
[i]<Sea Power>\BepInEx\config\dev.sewerlabs.seapowerradio.cfg[/i]. Every feature can be turned off: stock voice replacement, volume, background traffic, radio noise, callsign on every call, held and clickable messages, contact timings, neutral contacts, refusals, wingman VID, order queue and its key, fuel calls, RTB colour, SAR calls, unit roles, logging.

[h2]Requirements and compatibility[/h2]
[b]Anchor Chain[/b] is required: [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3380210757]subscribe[/url] and follow its [url=https://seapower-modders.github.io/AnchorChain/install-anchor-chain.html]installation guide[/url].
Sea Power 0.8.x. The voices come with the mod (about 390 MB), no internet needed. Voice lines from other mods play as before. If a game update moves something the mod hooks into, only that feature turns off and the log says which one. No game files are changed. Works with Rats Topographic Map and Rats Pop-Out Windows.

[h2]Problems?[/h2]
Please post [i]<Sea Power>\BepInEx\LogOutput.log[/i] with your report. It lists the roster, every call and every refusal.

[h2]Credits[/h2]
Voices rendered with Chatterbox from speakers of the CSTR VCTK Corpus: Yamagishi, Veaux, MacDonald (2019), University of Edinburgh, The Centre for Speech Technology Research, [url=https://doi.org/10.7488/ds/2645]doi.org/10.7488/ds/2645[/url], licensed [url=https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/]CC BY 4.0[/url]. The voice clips are CC BY 4.0 as well. Built on BepInEx, HarmonyX and Anchor Chain.

Source code (MIT): [url=https://github.com/struckl/SeaPowerRadio]github.com/struckl/SeaPowerRadio[/url]
